WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.790
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

7
00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.410
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

8
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.570
We're The Office ladies.

9
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.150
Good afternoon.

10
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:27.690
Good evening.

11
00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.050
You did it.

12
00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:29.870
You covered it all.

13
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:30.730
Did I get everybody?

14
00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:31.430
You did.

15
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:35.530
This week, we're talking about new boss.

16
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.890
I was delighted by this episode.

17
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:38.730
Me too.

18
00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.090
I read the script.

19
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:42.950
I read the script, and I was cackling.

20
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.370
Josh was like, what are you reading?

21
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.870
I'm like, it's the shooting draft for new boss.

22
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.070
It was so good.

23
00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.590
This was season five, episode 20.

24
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:58.990
It was written by Lee Eisenberg and Gene Stupnitsky and directed by Paul Feig.

25
00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.570
Hit me with a summary.

26
00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:01.390
I will.

27
00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.790
There is a new boss at corporate.

28
00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:11.210
They filled the position that used to be held by Jan, and then Ryan, with new regional

29
00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.510
supervisor, Charles Minor.

30
00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.370
And he's coming to Scranton for a visit.

31
00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.730
He's going to hobnob with us all day.

32
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:19.310
Yeah.

33
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:26.910
He is also going to announce some new policies involving our budget, in particular, our party

34
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:27.330
budget.

35
00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:32.670
Well, to be fair to the new boss, Michael was supposed to let us know.

36
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:33.930
He was.

37
00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.290
But once again, he did not.

38
00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:41.530
And instead, he started planning his own 15th anniversary party.

39
00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.330
He's been working at Dunder Mifflin for 15 years, and he wants to have a big party.

40
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:46.710
Big, big party.

41
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.570
Things do not go well with Michael and Charles, or with Jim and Charles.

42
00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:53.310
Oh, that was so fun.

43
00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:57.190
It's maybe one of my favorite runners ever.

44
00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:03.630
Watching Jim be sort of like on the outs with someone and feeling like embarrassed was

45
00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.770
so good.

46
00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.090
So, so fun.

47
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.330
Michael is spinning out.

48
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.530
He tries to get David Wallace on the phone.

49
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.330
He can't.

50
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:18.290
So, he drives to New York, where things are going to take a dramatic turn.

51
00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.210
Bum, bum, bum.

52
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:21.250
Fast fact number one.

53
00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:21.910
Mm-hmm.

54
00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.910
I mentioned there's a new boss in town.

55
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.950
Oh, please give me the character breakdown.

56
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.270
That person is Idris Elba.

57
00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:30.850
Yep.

58
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:31.970
All right.

59
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.050
Yes, it is.

60
00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.830
Let's get this out of the way.

61
00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.430
Let's just roll up our sleeves and talk about Idris for a second.

62
00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:38.870
Idris?

63
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.050
Is the most beautiful man I have ever laid my eyes on.

64
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:43.530
He is.

65
00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:47.670
And I want to say I've laid my eyes on some gentleman, famous gentleman.

66
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:48.190
Mm-hmm.

67
00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:49.730
He's not just beautiful.

68
00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:53.510
There is just, I don't know how to explain it, you guys.

69
00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:57.090
When, you know those people that walk in a room and you just go, damn.

70
00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:58.630
It's a whole aura.

71
00:02:59.010 --> 00:02:59.410
Yeah.

72
00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.430
It's a presence.

73
00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:04.730
We got a fan question from Anna B and Allie C.

74
00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.250
How did Idris Elba end up on the show?

75
00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:12.150
Randy told me that the role of Charles Minor was written for Idris.

76
00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.990
He is the only person they ever approached for the role.

77
00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:20.710
He said that Idris's name was in the story outline for this episode, which comes out like

78
00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.790
two months before you even shoot it.

79
00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.470
And he even sent it to me.

80
00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:30.510
It says, interior reception, Charles Minor, comma, Idris enters.

81
00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:34.310
Like, they were like, we want Idris Elba for this role.

82
00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:37.130
Oh my gosh, they were planning this pretty far out.

83
00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:37.470
Yeah.

84
00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.310
Well, the writing staff was a really huge fan of the TV show, The Wire.

85
00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.330
That's what I was going to say.

86
00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.150
We had a lot of The Wire fans.

87
00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:45.590
Do you remember that show?

88
00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.190
Everybody was so obsessed with it.

89
00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.110
It's such a great show.

90
00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:48.310
Yeah.

91
00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.830
And to get Stringer Bell, come on.

92
00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:51.650
I know.

93
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:56.610
Well, he's the second cast member we've had from The Wire because Amy Ryan was also on

94
00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:56.990
The Wire.

95
00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:57.250
Yep.

96
00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:03.250
We got another fan question from Derek H, who said, I've always wondered how it works with

97
00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:09.250
actors who have one accent in real life, but have to speak in a different accent for

98
00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:09.750
the role.

99
00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.710
Did Idris Elba speak in his normal British accent between takes, or did he stay in character

100
00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.830
with his American accent the whole time?

101
00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.310
Yeah, I guess we should mention Idris has a British accent.

102
00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.830
Which pretty much wrecked us all.

103
00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:27.310
Yeah.

104
00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:32.310
I mean, if you look like Idris and then you sound like Idris, come on.

105
00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:33.830
What were we supposed to do?

106
00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:36.070
We were all so charmed by him.

107
00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:37.530
So charmed.

108
00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.730
And yes, he did speak in his British accent between takes.

109
00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:41.590
Right.

110
00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:45.270
Fast fact number two, more Idris Elba.

111
00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:45.870
Okay.

112
00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:46.630
I'm sorry.

113
00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:47.870
He's too great.

114
00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:49.350
We're going to keep talking about him.

115
00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:55.210
As you know, since being on The Wire and The Office, he has gone on to do a gazillion

116
00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.950
things, movies, TV shows.

117
00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:59.090
He was most recently in The Suicide Squad.

118
00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:00.870
But did you know this?

119
00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.490
Because I didn't know this.

120
00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.570
Before he was an actor, he was a DJ.

121
00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:06.850
Okay.

122
00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.030
I did know that.

123
00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:13.070
I did know that probably because I was so excited he was coming.

124
00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:14.770
I like looked up everything about him.

125
00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:17.710
Just in case a topic came up, I would know something.

126
00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:18.810
Wait.

127
00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:24.590
Before he was on the show, you like Googled him and you're like, I need topics of conversation.

128
00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.790
I just want to know everything about him.

129
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:27.750
Okay.

130
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:33.370
I did not Google him until we had to prep for this podcast, during which I found out

131
00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.150
that he is a big time DJ.

132
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:35.770
Yeah.

133
00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.270
Growing up, he DJed parties.

134
00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:42.770
And when he was a struggling actor and he first came to the US, it was really hard for

135
00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.430
him to get work because he had a special visa.

136
00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.990
But he was able to get gigs as a DJ.

137
00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.730
He also worked as a bouncer, which I found very interesting.

138
00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.370
He goes by the name of DJ Big Driss.

139
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.630
And now he's done like tons of shows.

140
00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.110
He did a party for Michael Jordan.

141
00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.330
He DJed the wedding of Prince Harry and Meghan Markle.

142
00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:01.970
That I did not know.

143
00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.230
How did I miss that?

144
00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.110
You need to do more Googling.

145
00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:06.830
Well, I guess I need to re-Google.

146
00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:07.450
Yeah.

147
00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.650
He also played Coachello in 2019.

148
00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.090
He got rave reviews.

149
00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:18.390
And he just released a new house track through his label, Seven Wallace, called Fudge, with

150
00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.430
vocalist Eliza Legzina.

151
00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:21.850
And I thought we should listen to it.

152
00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:22.650
Oh, I'd love that.

153
00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:42.270
This man can do no wrong.

154
00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:43.390
He, I mean.

155
00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:44.350
Stop it.

156
00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.530
Can we get invited to a party where he DJs?

157
00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:47.510
It'll never happen.

158
00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:48.450
Can we?

159
00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:49.590
I don't think it'll happen.

160
00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.030
I don't think we'll make the liSt.

161
00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.270
But I would love it.

162
00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:53.030
You know what?

163
00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.210
Let's go hide in some bushes.

164
00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:55.970
We'll sneak in.

165
00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:57.090
Bush hiders.

166
00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:58.110
We'll push hiders.

167
00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.630
Bush hiders goes to the club.

168
00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:07.550
Lady, this is like kind of one of my new life goals, which is to go to a party that Idris

169
00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:08.410
DJs with you.

170
00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:09.470
I want to do it.

171
00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:10.630
And dance it up.

172
00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:11.730
What do we wear?

173
00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.290
What do you wear to something like that?

174
00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:15.530
Can I wear one of my fake flowers?

175
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:16.910
I'm going to say no.

176
00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:17.730
Not on this.

177
00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:18.210
I agree.

178
00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:18.850
I agree.

179
00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.830
You know what show that Idris was in that my husband loved was Luther.

180
00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:23.450
Yes.

181
00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:24.930
Luther is so good.

182
00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.010
It's so good.

183
00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:31.790
He also did a show that he created himself where he plays a DJ called Turn Up Charlie.

184
00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:33.730
He's done it all.

185
00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:34.510
He's done it all.

186
00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:35.450
All right.

187
00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:36.450
I will move us on.

188
00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.250
Fast fact number three is not about Idris, even though it could be.

189
00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:44.150
Fast fact number three is, why is it raining in this episode?

190
00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:45.770
All throughout the episode.

191
00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:46.750
The whole episode.

192
00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:47.030
Pouring rain.

193
00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:47.930
Not just raining.

194
00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:48.530
Pouring rain.

195
00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:51.050
So much so you can hear it in the talking heads.

196
00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:52.030
Yeah.

197
00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:52.510
Yeah.

198
00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:54.730
I started by looking in the script.

199
00:07:54.890 --> 00:08:00.110
I thought, was there a deleted something that tells us why it's raining?

200
00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:04.670
I only found one random side comment by Michael.

201
00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:05.310
Same.

202
00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:08.790
It's in the scene where he's making small talk with Charles.

203
00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:09.450
Yep.

204
00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:12.810
And he says, so great weather we're having, huh?

205
00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:14.770
Kind of rainy, you know, but still humid.

206
00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:16.490
But that was it.

207
00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:17.330
That is it.

208
00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:19.650
So I asked Randy about it and here's what he said.

209
00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:26.370
He said, if I recall correctly, we entered this week expecting rain all across LA.

210
00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:32.010
In fact, the top of our call sheets for the first two days of shooting say, be prepared

211
00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.870
for rain because they would do that.

212
00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:38.430
And then they would have extra umbrellas and like all kinds of things for us if we were

213
00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:39.170
shooting outside.

214
00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:44.650
Randy said the very first day of shooting, we had to shoot these driving scenes with

215
00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:45.030
Steve.

216
00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:50.470
They were supposed to be Michael driving to and from his meeting with David Wallace.

217
00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:52.230
Oh, I have some.

218
00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.750
I'm going to play later for when he leaves.

219
00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:55.550
It's amazing.

220
00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:57.330
Because they got deleted, right?

221
00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:57.910
That's right.

222
00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:02.910
Well, I guess while they were shooting this, it was raining all over the car.

223
00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:04.790
The wipers are going and all that.

224
00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:10.930
That afternoon, they moved back to our parking lot where they had to shoot the scenes of Angela

225
00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:12.770
and Kelly trying to find Charles.

226
00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.310
That's one, Angela, where you're like gripping his scarf.

227
00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:15.790
Yeah, yeah.

228
00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:16.430
In the rain.

229
00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:16.930
Yes.

230
00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.970
He said it was steadily raining the whole afternoon.

231
00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.110
It was perfect.

232
00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:24.190
It became a whole bit, which I know we can get to later.

233
00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.810
But it made the scene that much more fun.

234
00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:31.770
It's funny because when I first saw it, I asked Randy, did you guys spray down the parking

235
00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.030
lot with water?

236
00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:35.610
And he said, no, that was our rainy day.

237
00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.530
Yes, it was actually raining.

238
00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:42.390
And it was one of the only times I filmed on the office where they didn't care about my

239
00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:42.990
hair and makeup.

240
00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.730
They were just like, let her get wet.

241
00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:51.350
Well, I guess after we finished shooting that one day, it was necessary to match that

242
00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.230
to the rest of the episode.

243
00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:58.730
So Michael Gallenberg and our construction coordinator, Tim James, and the special effects

244
00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:05.170
crew led by Ron Neri added plexiglass sheets over all of the office windows, and they made

245
00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:09.030
it rain out every window for the rest of the episode.

246
00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:13.430
Well, I think that's so great because I think it would have been weird to have some scenes

247
00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:14.850
where it was raining and some not.

248
00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.450
I like the consistency.

249
00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:20.270
I also kind of loved it that we never talked about it.

250
00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:20.710
Yeah.

251
00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:23.030
Well, remember what you said about how you could hear it?

252
00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:29.510
I guess that was kind of an issue because our sound mixer, Ben Patrick, could really

253
00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.690
hear it through the microphones, especially in the back of the talking heads.

254
00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:40.890
So they tried putting down these big fuzzy mats called horse hairs inside of the metal troughs

255
00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:43.930
that the water would drip into, and then it would get sucked back up, and that would

256
00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:48.390
just sort of cycle through the window pane to try to quiet the water, but it didn't work

257
00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:48.910
100%.

258
00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:52.770
So you can hear the rain in the back of some of these scenes.

259
00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:58.690
Randy also wanted me to tell people that the fuzzy mats are not actually made out of horse

260
00:10:58.690 --> 00:10:58.910
hair.

261
00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:00.050
That was my next question.

262
00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.010
I said, oh no.

263
00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:01.730
Oh no.

264
00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:03.070
Why are they called that?

265
00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:03.490
Ah!

266
00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:09.350
Randy said that it's, quote, just another old-timey holdover from early Hollywood.

267
00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:12.570
Well, thank goodness, because I was worried for a second.

268
00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:13.610
It's fakie.

269
00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:14.470
Fakie hair.

270
00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:15.430
Fakie hair.

271
00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:18.150
Well, that's all I got, lady.

272
00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:19.670
Well, I loved all of that.

273
00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:21.950
We should probably take a break and then get into this episode.

274
00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:22.950
I think so.

275
00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.270
I've invented a new phrase for today.

276
00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:26.450
Oh, yeah?

277
00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:26.950
Uh-huh.

278
00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:27.970
You want to hear it?

279
00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:28.370
Yeah.

280
00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:30.890
It's called the fan mail flurry.

281
00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:31.970
Oh!

282
00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:36.590
And that's when we get tons of mail about a certain one subject in an episode.

283
00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:39.410
And this week, there were two fan mail flurries.

284
00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:45.130
Jenna coined a phrase, well, I want you to know, I had my husband make a sting for this

285
00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:45.590
episode.

286
00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:48.690
And I will surprise you with it.

287
00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:51.070
Let's take a break and we'll be back with new boss.

288
00:11:51.310 --> 00:12:06.440
We're going to kick off this episode with a little meeting in the conference room about

289
00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:09.340
Michael's 15th anniversary celebration.

290
00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:13.360
And who is in the party planning committee now?

291
00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.680
Jim, Dwight, and Pam.

292
00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:18.500
Oh, I'm just there to take notes.

293
00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.320
I don't think I'm really in it.

294
00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:23.840
Although later, I guess I am throwing out ideas.

295
00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:24.760
You are.

296
00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:25.400
Hmm.

297
00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:26.700
How'd I make my way back in?

298
00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:28.400
I think Jim wrangled you in.

299
00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.340
And I'm going to say, traitor.

300
00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:39.840
Dwight is going to pitch the idea of a 15-minute round of applause followed by a 15-minute moment

301
00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:40.420
of silence.

302
00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:44.280
What would that 30 minutes be like if you're an audience member?

303
00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:46.340
It's 30 minutes total.

304
00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:48.880
It's so hilarious to me.

305
00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:52.240
Like, I know we could never have done this, but I would have loved to film that.

306
00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:56.100
And I would have loved for all of us to have had to clap for 15 minutes.

307
00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:57.720
That would be so difficult.

308
00:12:58.000 --> 00:12:58.580
I know.

309
00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:00.340
And then try watching it.

310
00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.120
Well, the camera pans over, and Jim is wearing a tuxedo.

311
00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:09.640
And wow, this is really going to haunt him later.

312
00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.320
He's wearing a tuxedo.

313
00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:12.680
He slicked his hair.

314
00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:13.320
Mm-hmm.

315
00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:17.660
He went all out to rub it in Dwight's face about his dress code memo.

316
00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:23.640
Dwight is going to continue to list off activities that Jim is sort of shooting down.

317
00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.480
Jim is clearly influencing Michael, right?

318
00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:26.920
Mm-hmm.

319
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:33.420
So Dwight says, a string quartet performing classy Cal music, an ice sculpture surrounded

320
00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:34.840
by chocolate-covered strawberries.

321
00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:36.940
An ice sculpture of Michael.

322
00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:37.600
Yeah.

323
00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:39.360
This scene would have continued.

324
00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:40.860
Part of it was edited out.

325
00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.340
In the script, Jim says, you can't buy classy.

326
00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:45.460
You can't teach classy.

327
00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:46.240
You're born with it.

328
00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:47.280
Michael has it.

329
00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:48.100
Pam has it.

330
00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:49.420
Dwight goes, really?

331
00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:50.580
Pam has it?

332
00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:55.560
And Michael says, Pam has class coming out of her ass.

333
00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:56.540
Wow.

334
00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.460
Pam says, thank you, Michael.

335
00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:00.760
That was very classy of you to say.

336
00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.560
And then Dwight's like, well, I'm sorry I don't know what classy is.

337
00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:05.840
That is amazing.

338
00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:06.820
I love that.

339
00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:13.160
You know, in this meeting, one of the things they're going to decide is classy is Mr. Peanut.

340
00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:15.040
Yes.

341
00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:18.820
Mr. Peanut is the logo for the Planter's Peanut Company.

342
00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:19.360
Mm-hmm.

343
00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:21.460
Which, listen to this.

344
00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:21.900
What?

345
00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:26.780
Was founded in 1906 in Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania.

346
00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:28.420
Oh, that's awesome.

347
00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:29.580
How about that?

348
00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.020
And I have more to say about Mr. Peanut.

349
00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:33.020
What?

350
00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:39.920
This peanut is like one of the best known, most iconic images in advertising history.

351
00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:40.420
Oh, yeah.

352
00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:41.700
I have it in my mind instantly.

353
00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:42.100
Yes.

354
00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:47.280
When you say Mr. Peanut, I picture him with his hat and he has a cane and a little monocle.

355
00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:47.660
Yeah.

356
00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:48.560
You know it.

357
00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:48.840
Yeah.

358
00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:49.660
You know Mr. Peanut.

359
00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:52.200
He kind of kicks one leg over the other when he stands.

360
00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:52.920
He does.

361
00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:53.300
Yeah.

362
00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.400
So here's where he came from.

363
00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:01.860
They did a contest to have someone design a logo for the company.

364
00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:10.320
And a young schoolboy by the name of Antonio Gentile submitted a drawing of an anthropomorphic peanut.

365
00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.680
And he won the contest and he got $5.

366
00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:15.320
$5.

367
00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:15.980
$5.

368
00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:23.640
But I'll have you know that the founder of the company, his name was Amidio Obici, if I'm saying that correctly.

369
00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:30.200
He was so taken with this little boy that he ended up paying his way through college and all of his siblings' way through college.

370
00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:31.480
Oh, I like hearing that.

371
00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:35.720
So I guess he got $5 and a college education for himself and his siblings.

372
00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:42.380
There was a commercial artist named Andrew Wallach who added the monocle, the top hat, and the cane.

373
00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:44.380
So it was a bit of a...

374
00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:45.060
Two-hander.

375
00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:45.900
A two-hander.

376
00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:48.700
However, the plot thickens.

377
00:15:49.340 --> 00:15:55.860
There is a disputed claim that a different artist actually added the monocle, top hat, and cane.

378
00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:58.800
The family of Frank P.

379
00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:01.580
Creasy claims that he did it.

380
00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:05.960
He was a Wilkes-Barre artist and also the head of the Suffolk planter plant.

381
00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:07.440
They think he did it.

382
00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.720
So, Mom Detective, what happened?

383
00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:11.640
Thank you, Angela.

384
00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:19.080
There are no sources still in circulation that can positively identify the artiSt.

385
00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:21.600
We know the little boy started it all.

386
00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.480
But who added the monocle, top hat, and cane?

387
00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:25.640
We'll never know.

388
00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.520
We'll never really know.

389
00:16:27.700 --> 00:16:28.380
There you go.

390
00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:30.200
On this episode of Mom Detective.

391
00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:36.340
Is that going to be unsatisfying if the episodes of Mom Detective end with, we'll never know?

392
00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:38.500
I think many of them will in that way.

393
00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.120
Well, I have a little thing to share about the script.

394
00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:42.760
Tell me.

395
00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.660
Because, you know, I loved the shooting draft of this script.

396
00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:54.800
And Jenna and I have talked to you guys about how we would have a scene, and then sometimes in the moment, we'd play around with it, and it would change.

397
00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:55.820
Right there in the moment.

398
00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.740
So, I think this is a great example.

399
00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:02.160
And I don't think our show could have been like this without Steve Carell.

400
00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:03.440
I'll tell you why.

401
00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:09.560
In the shooting draft, Michael is the one that has the whole speech about Mr. Peanut, not Jim.

402
00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:14.940
Michael says, this needs to be classy, like the grand opening of a car dealership or Mr. Peanut.

403
00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:17.740
And then Dwight says, Mr. Peanut's not classy.

404
00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:21.320
He's just a regular peanut dressed in a top hat and cane.

405
00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:23.380
And Michael says, that's what makes him classy.

406
00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:26.660
But when you watch the episode, it's a two-hander.

407
00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:29.800
Michael says, we need it to be classy like a car dealership.

408
00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.320
Jim says, or Mr. Peanut.

409
00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:34.980
But, and it's a two-hander, and it makes the scene so much better.

410
00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:38.080
Because then Michael and Jim are kind of in cahoots about this, right?

411
00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:39.700
And Dwight's the odd man out.

412
00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:41.500
I bet that happened on the day.

413
00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:41.880
Yeah.

414
00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:43.080
That's so great.

415
00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:43.860
Isn't that cool?

416
00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:49.740
I know these are sort of tiny moments, but I just think it speaks to our creative collaboration on the show.

417
00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:50.440
Absolutely.

418
00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:55.480
Angela, this cold open also created Fan Mail Flurry number one.

419
00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:56.240
Okay.

420
00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.060
I feel like I'd need a sting.

421
00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:01.040
You mentioned sting, and now I want a Fan Mail Flurry.

422
00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:04.360
Like stuff going into an inbox.

423
00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:13.300
People have a lot to say about whether or not this is Michael's 15th year at Dunder Mifflin or not.

424
00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:14.280
Ooh.

425
00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.340
Let me get you started with Maddie L.

426
00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:19.600
Fan Mail, Fan Mail, Fan Mail Flurry!

427
00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:32.300
Maddie says, per this episode's air date, Michael would have started work at Dunder Mifflin on March 19th, 1994.

428
00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:45.880
But during Michael's last Dundies, the cast sings the song 9,986,000 minutes in tribute to how long he's worked at the company.

429
00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:55.060
And if you do the math from that air date, that would mean that Michael actually worked at Dunder Mifflin for 19 years.

430
00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:56.780
So much math.

431
00:18:57.020 --> 00:19:02.340
If you back it up to this episode, that would be 17 years at this point.

432
00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:03.440
Huh.

433
00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:04.920
Kelly S.

434
00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:07.220
got in on this and said,

435
00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:14.800
In the negotiation from season 3, Daryl says that Michael has been working at Dunder Mifflin for 14 years.

436
00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:20.280
This is two years later, so that would make this his 16th year.

437
00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:22.080
Mm-hmm.

438
00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:22.820
Mm-hmm.

439
00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:26.420
How long has Michael been working at Dunder Mifflin?

440
00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:27.200
It's shifting.

441
00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:29.940
15, 16, 17 years?

442
00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:39.280
This feels like one of those math word problems on a test, like about the train that goes to Pennsylvania, but then stops off in New York and blah, blah, blah.

443
00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:40.460
Guys.

444
00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:48.320
I've seen teachers do that, where they'll do a math word problem using office characters and stuff.

445
00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:49.440
I think that's really fun.

446
00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:50.120
I do, too.

447
00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:50.800
All right.

448
00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:52.260
I still wouldn't be good at it, though.

449
00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:54.660
No, because it's still math.

450
00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:55.680
Bingo.

451
00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:57.980
Were you good in math in school?

452
00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:07.960
Because I was very good at math in school, and now, I mean, everyone knows my calculator story where I tried to add without a calculator for a year, and I don't know.

453
00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:08.840
It's left me.

454
00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:12.460
Like, I can barely help my 10-year-old with his math homework now.

455
00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:13.340
Well.

456
00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:14.160
What happened to me?

457
00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:15.320
I mean, I was really good.

458
00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:20.380
I was like, you know, 98th percentile in the math tests and stuff.

459
00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:30.460
I was a solid, like, B student in math, totally fine, all the way up to algebra 2, and then I had the option to take language over trig, and I love language.

460
00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:40.100
I love speaking other languages, and so I actually went all the way up to Spanish 4, but I was able to opt out of trig to do language.

461
00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:40.960
I loved trigonometry.

462
00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:42.020
I didn't take it.

463
00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:46.940
I didn't care for geometry, but I loved trigonometry, algebra, all of that.

464
00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:47.480
Yeah.

465
00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:49.120
Yeah, but it has left me.

466
00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:57.860
Let's get back to the episode, because a gentleman named Charles has arrived, and he would like to speak with Michael.

467
00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:02.520
Hold up, but first, I have a very tall plant alert.

468
00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:03.860
What's happening?

469
00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:07.260
We haven't had a plant at reception alert in a long time.

470
00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:09.180
Two minutes, 23 seconds.

471
00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:11.940
This might be Pam's tallest potted plant.

472
00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:13.540
I just want to go on record.

473
00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:15.040
You know, it's interesting.

474
00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:19.960
I was just looking at the watercolor that Morgan did of Pam's plants.

475
00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:20.280
Yes.

476
00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:23.460
There is a plant on there that's very, very tall.

477
00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:24.840
It's down in the corner.

478
00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.340
It's so tall, it actually encroaches on one of the other plants.

479
00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:30.020
Could this be that plant?

480
00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:31.120
Maybe it is.

481
00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:35.460
The watercolor painting of Pam's plants that we're talking about is done by this fantastic artiSt.

482
00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:36.260
Her name is Morgan.

483
00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:40.060
You can find her on Instagram, Morgan Giselle Art.

484
00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:42.160
Morgan G-E-S-E-L-L-A-R-T.

485
00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:44.800
You guys, she's painted all of Pam's plants.

486
00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:46.700
She calls it Beasley's Botanicals.

487
00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:52.600
You can get it as a mug, and she was kind enough to gift me with the original watercolor, which I treasure.

488
00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:54.000
I know, and you know what she did?

489
00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.640
She did all of Angela Martin's outfits in a watercolor painting.

490
00:21:57.800 --> 00:21:59.240
I have it hanging in my bedroom.

491
00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:01.980
So, yes, there is a very tall plant at reception.

492
00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:08.300
I didn't notice the plant, Angela, because my eyes were on Idris Elba entering the room.

493
00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.720
Yes, Sir Charles Minor has arrived.

494
00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:16.040
At 2 minutes 50 seconds, Angela Martin looks him up and down.

495
00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:26.460
Michael welcomes him in and says that everybody needs to go into the break room because he has a surprise that he is setting up.

496
00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:29.080
Yeah, he whispers to Dwight, I have to get the fish ready.

497
00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:29.980
Yep.

498
00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:30.720
Gross.

499
00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:31.260
What?

500
00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:35.260
And now for a segment I'd like to call...

501
00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:40.900
History in the break room.

502
00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:42.380
What?

503
00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:46.480
Break room.

504
00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:50.260
I made my husband do that, like, last night in his car.

505
00:22:50.260 --> 00:22:50.940
You can tell.

506
00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:52.840
I'm like, do it in the car, in the minivan.

507
00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:53.820
It'll sound better.

508
00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:56.480
History in the break room.

509
00:22:56.760 --> 00:23:00.520
So, Michael needs Dwight to distract Charles and the group.

510
00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:01.780
They go in the break room.

511
